AB  - The rapid evolution of large language models (LLMs) has presented significant opportunities for the automated generation of Infrastructure as Code (IaC), particularly for Terraform. However, when handling declarative languages with strict logical dependencies, existing approaches often struggle to bridge the semantic gap between ambiguous user instructions and precise cloud resource configurations. To address this challenge, this paper proposes a Terraform code generation framework based on Dual-layer Graph Retrieval-Augmented Generation (DG-RAG). The framework restructures the conventional generation pipeline into three stages. First, during intent decomposition, an intent orchestration graph is introduced to retrieve high-quality reference architectural cases, guiding the LLM in macro-level task decomposition and resource planning. Second, the retrieved graph substructures are utilized to conduct a targeted secondary retrieval within an underlying resource graph, enabling micro-level parameter completion and reducing syntactic errors. Finally, the architectural intent and code constraints are assembled to prompt the LLM to generate compliant HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL) code. To evaluate the practical applicability of the proposed system, comprehensive ablation studies and cross-model experiments were conducted on the IaC-Eval dataset. Experimental results demonstrate that, compared to conventional baseline systems, the proposed method improves the execution success rate by 62% in complex architectural scenarios, while resource coverage and required parameter coverage increase by 53.5% and 38.%, respectively. Ultimately, this framework effectively mitigates semantic omissions and enhances the reliability and generalizability of LLM-based Terraform generation.
ER  - 

