AB  - The rapid mainstream adoption of generative AI has created a practical requirement for application-level platforms that can operationalize large language models (LLMs) safely, economically, and at scale, while still providing a user experience that is understandable to non-expert users. In response to this need, this paper presents AetherAI, a modular, web-based, subscriptiongoverned multimodal content creation platform engineered to provide text generation, code generation, image generation, conversational assistance, website-content analysis chat, document-assisted chat, and speech modalities (speech-to-text and text-to-speech) through a unified orchestration layer. The platform is designed around a clear separation of concerns: (i) an HTTP routing and middleware perimeter for installation gating, rolebased access, and request sanitization; (ii) controller-driven feature entry points aligned with user tasks; (iii) a dedicated service/wrapper layer that centralizes OpenAI API interaction and abstracts provider-specific request formats; and (iv) a persistence layer that records generations, chat history, and subscription usage to enforce plan policies. The system emphasizes repeatable feature behavior and maintainable extension by concentrating model selection, API credentials, and generation policies into a single configuration surface, and by using specialized pipelines per tool type (templates, chat, images, TTS, STT) rather than a single monolithic prompt style. This paper details the platform's architecture, request life cycle, data governance decisions (planbased quotas and usage tracking), security boundary controls (installer lock, admin/user guards, sanitization), and the design implications of integrating multiple AI modalities in one product. The contribution is a complete, code-grounded account of a production-oriented LLM platform architecture suitable for academic evaluation and future enhancement through retrieval augmentation, policy enforcement hardening, and model-provider interchangeability.
