AB  - Anti-pattens are quick solutions which have negative consequences or cause significant risks in software development. Anti-patterns cause low maintainability, difficult to refactor and high technical debt. This paper uses Large Language Models (LLM) to detect anti-patterns in software development lifecycle to save time, improve modularity, clear code structure and reusability in turn reducing technical debt. The work addresses two key challenges in applying LLMs to program analysis: robust machine-readable output generation, and efficient repeatable retrieval-augmented prompting across large codebases. We introduce a unified detection pipeline that combines a RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) knowledge base built from curated examples and static-analysis artifacts with prompt engineering and schema-driven parsing to produce line-level anti-pattern detections. Methodologically, the system constructs language-aware prompts incorporating highsimilarity examples retrieved via a sentence transformer embedding and FAISS (Facebook AI Similarity Search) index. To improve reliability, the detector enforces a JSON-first output contract (pattern, lines, confidence, rationale). Empirical evaluation uses a multi-dimensional framework that measures success rate, detection coverage, explainability, throughput, and cross-model consistency. Across evaluated models, a clear trade-off emerged: larger reasoning or safety-tuned models produced richer but slower analyses, while lighter instruction models delivered higher throughput with simpler pattern taxonomies. We think our understandings will inspire future work on anti-patterns detection systems.
