AB  - Large language model (LLM) code generation is increasingly adopted, with developers employing different LLM interaction strategies from basic prompts to various context-rich strategies. This study empirically compares three strategies, Basic Prompting, Context Engineering, and Specification-Driven Development, on a controlled web application testbed. Nine code versions were evaluated using functional testing, static analysis via SonarQube, static application security testing (SAST) via CodeQL, and dynamic application security testing (DAST) via ZAP. While all versions achieved 100% functional correctness, confirming that all three strategies can generate specification-aligned implementations under controlled conditions, trade-offs emerged in non-functional dimensions. Basic Prompting produced the strongest SonarQube structural profile, Context Engineering achieved the lowest duplication but highest CodeQL alerts, and Specification-Driven Development delivered zero CodeQL alerts but mixed SonarQube results. ZAP recorded zero blocking failures across all versions, with finding dominated by passive security hardening warnings, mostly from shared framework-level hardening gaps. Overall, no single strategy consistently outperformed the others across all dimensions, demonstrating that AI-generated code quality depends on how interaction strategies shape structural quality, security posture, and maintainability.
ER  - 

