AB  - Custom GPTs allow creators to publish configurable large language model assistants by combining hidden system-level instructions with optional uploaded knowledge, concentrating sensitive creator-provided assets behind a conversational interface that is not a hardened security boundary. We measure confidentiality risk in store-prominent Custom GPTs under a prompt-only adversary model and focus on identity-spoofing prompt injection, where a user impersonates a privileged role (e.g., the creator) and frames disclosure as benign verification or debugging. Using a fixed four-stage protocol on a mid-May 2025 store snapshot (N=57), we observe unauthorised system-instruction disclosure in 87.7% (50/57) of targets, with consistently high rates across store categories. Conditional on Stage-1 success, 28/50 targets further exposed knowledge-base metadata and/or content when a knowledge base was available, indicating that instruction leakage can enable downstream disclosure steps. We further provide an evidence trace showing how identity claims and verification-style framing can shift targets into a compliance-oriented mode and accelerate cross-stage disclosure. Overall, the results position identity-spoofing as a distinct, reproducible prompt-injection strategy in store-prominent Custom GPTs and motivate layered mitigations spanning creator configuration and platform-level leakage monitoring.
ER  - 

