AB  - Recent advancements in code generation have shifted the focus from isolated functions to class-level methods enriched with contextual information. While benchmarks like ClassEval and CoderEval address such contexts, the specific impact of method-level factors, such as descriptions, intermodule interactions (coupling), and project context-remains underexplored. Our study investigates method-level code generation in ObjectOriented Programming (OOP), evaluating how varying prompt contexts, from method-only to project-wide-affect performance and cost. We propose a novel metric, Prompt-Token CostEffectiveness, to assess the trade-off between contextual richness and token usage. Results show that prompts including method invocation details offer the best cost-effectiveness. Furthermore, LLMs differ in error patterns and developer support: larger models are not always superior. High-coupling tasks pose greater challenges, with GPT-4 performing best in low-coupling scenarios, and GPT-3.5 excelling in high-coupling cases. These findings suggest that careful prompt design and model selection can significantly improve both code quality and economic efficiency.
