AB  - Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) have enabled increasingly complex code generation with high syntactic fluency. Despite this, LLMs frequently hallucinate—producing code that appears logically correct yet fails during execution or mishandles critical edge cases. This limitation is particularly severe for Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA) problems, where execution-level correctness and reasoning consistency are essential. This paper presents COM (Cost-efficient Open-source Multi-agent), a framework that mitigates hallucinations and enhances reasoning reliability in zero-shot code generation, and GenEval, an industry-oriented DSA benchmark curated from competitive programming platforms and real-world practitioner problems, publicly released on HuggingFace. COM decomposes code generation into four specialized agents—Creator, Critic, Verifier, and Refiner—coordinated through an execution-grounded critique–verification–refinement cycle to approximate self-corrective behavior absent in single-pass systems. Evaluated on HumanEval and GenEval using Pass@k and hallucination rate, COM achieves an 8.05% Pass@k improvement with 34.87% relative hallucination reduction on HumanEval, and 5.77% improvement with 27.37% reduction on GenEval. GenEval is established as a reproducible benchmark for systematic evaluation of reasoning failures in code-centric LLM applications, and has been adopted by 18+ researchers within three weeks of its public release.
ER  - 

