AB  - As large language models (LLMs) are now widely deployed in natural language understanding, program synthesis and interactive dialogue systems, the security risks they introduce have attracted growing attention. A central concern is jailbreak attacks, which deliberately bypass safety alignment mechanisms and can coerce models into producing harmful or policy breaching content, thereby threatening both system reliability and user safety. This work presents a comprehensive survey of jailbreak attacks against LLMs and multimodal large language models (MLLMs). We organize existing techniques according to the adversary’s access level into white-box and black-box settings. In the white-box case, attackers exploit gradient signals or detailed model information to construct adversarial prompts, whereas black-box attacks rely solely on query interactions or carefully crafted contextual cues to elicit unsafe behavior. With the emergence of MLLMs, research has expanded from text based environments to multimodal scenarios such as vision language applications. This paper reviews representative methods and their development over time, examines the mechanisms that enable jailbreaks and highlights key challenges in maintaining alignment and ensuring the security of multimodal systems.
ER  - 

